Fix: XSA extraction byproducts must not count as stale sources

`loadhw -hw <project>/x.xsa` makes xsdb unpack the XSA next to itself, which
drops a drivers/ tree and ps7_init.* into the Vivado project directory with a
fresh mtime on every run.

The staleness scan then sees those files as design sources newer than the
bitstream, so the next run reports "N source file(s) newer than the bitstream"
and refuses to program. Building cannot clear it: synth and impl really are up
to date, so the impl .bit keeps its old timestamp and the condition is
permanent. In other words a successful run makes the following run impossible.

Observed with a Zynq-7000 HLS design: 6 files under drivers/ plus 5 ps7_init.*
files, all extracted by loadhw, reported as 11 stale sources for ever after.

--- a/remote_agent.py
+++ b/remote_agent.py
@@
 SKIP_DIRS = re.compile(
-    r"(^|/)(\.git|\.Xil|\.jobs|ip_user_files|sim_\w+|\.metadata|_ide)(/|$)"
+    r"(^|/)(\.git|\.Xil|\.jobs|ip_user_files|sim_\w+|\.metadata|_ide|drivers)(/|$)"
 )

+# `loadhw` unpacks the XSA next to it, so ps7_init.* and a drivers/ tree land
+# in the project directory every time a run happens. They are extraction
+# byproducts of the tool's own flow, never design sources, and treating them as
+# sources makes each run invalidate the next one.
+SKIP_FILES = re.compile(r"(^|/)(ps7_init|psu_init)(_gpl)?\.(c|h|tcl|html)$")
+

@@ staleness scan
     for pattern in SOURCE_GLOBS:
         for path in rglob(project, pattern):
-            if SKIP_DIRS.search(path):
+            if SKIP_DIRS.search(path) or SKIP_FILES.search(path):
                 continue
